rrentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n <use xlink:href=\"#E10-MJMATHI-78\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\"></use>\n <use xlink:href=\"#E10-MJMAIN-3C\" x=\"850\" y=\"0\"></use>\n <use xlink:href=\"#E10-MJMAIN-2212\" x=\"1906\" y=\"0\"></use>\n<g transform=\"translate(2685,0)\">\n <use xlink:href=\"#E10-MJMAIN-31\"></use>\n <use xlink:href=\"#E10-MJMAIN-2E\" x=\"500\" y=\"0\"></use>\n</g>\n</g>\n</svg></span></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To graph this compound inequality, we first graph the two inequalities separately.</p>\n<div id=\"graphic-6223dd682da51dc129335dc33ff47ef1\" class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/6223dd682da51dc129335dc33ff47ef1\"></div>\n\n<p><br></p>\n<p>Then, we keep only where they overlap.</p>\n<div id=\"graphic-c1ab56b5765f8e0353c18be9269c2fcb\" class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/c1ab56b5765f8e0353c18be9269c2fcb\"></div>\n\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Any compound \"and\" inequality will always represent a segment between two points. So, it is not always necessary to draw both inequalities and find the overlap. To speed up the process, we can just draw the endpoints with open or closed circles as appropriate, and highlight the area between the endpoints.</p>\n           \n                </div>        \n                    \n            </div>",
